Seed appearance and experimental settings from the runtime

A freshly provisioned workspace could not declare how its Orca looks or which
features are on: every browser opening the tile started at stock defaults, and
the same toggles got re-clicked after every workspace create. The web client
keeps settings in localStorage, and of the 184 top-level `GlobalSettings` keys
on `v1.4.156` it pulls exactly five from the runtime - `theme` and the
`experimental*` flags are not among them, so writing `orca-data.json` on the
workspace changed nothing.

A shared allowlist of appearance and capability keys is now returned by
`settings.get` and adopted exactly once, on a browser's first visit, when
localStorage holds no settings blob. Nothing is written back and nothing is
re-imposed on later loads, so these are defaults rather than policy. Per-device
ergonomics (zoom, window bounds, font sizes) and credentials are excluded, and
values route through the app's own normalizers instead of rules restated here.

To test, set a seeded key in the workspace's `orca-data.json` and open the tile
in a browser with no Orca localStorage; then change it in the UI and reload to
confirm the workspace does not override the user. Covered by
`runtime-seeded-settings.test.ts` for the key picker, and
`web-preload-settings-seeding.test.ts` for the load path - the first-visit gate,
the exclusions measured end to end, and that nothing is written back.

A load that never reached the store does not spend the first visit: the blob
stays absent and the next load seeds. That is the gate holding rather than
leaking - a browser whose first open happened offline would otherwise be denied
the workspace's declaration permanently, with no recovery short of clearing site
data.

